WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.810
Here is a list of one million names

2
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.180
stored in a computer.

3
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.258
You ask for the five hundred

4
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.300
thousandth name.

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.370
It comes back instantly.

6
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.713
Did the computer count through five

7
00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.930
hundred thousand names to find it?

8
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.130
If it had

9
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.520
a list this long would be painfully slow.

10
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.230
So how does it jump straight there?

11
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.401
The answer is in how a list

12
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.780
is laid out in memory.

13
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.930
That's the array.

14
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.950
In this video we'll see what

15
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.310
memory looks like

16
00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.890
the one formula that makes jumping possible

17
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.320
and what that layout makes slow.

18
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.910
Memory is a very long row of boxes.

19
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.950
Each box holds one byte

20
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.460
and each has a number, its address.

21
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.620
To keep the pictures simple

22
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.920
we'll say an item takes four bytes

23
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.450
so it fills four boxes in a row.

24
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.970
An integer, a letter, a small record

25
00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.190
whatever you store

26
00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.600
it sits in boxes with consecutive addresses.

27
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.706
An array is the simplest

28
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.430
way to use that row.

29
00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.550
We reserve one block of boxes

30
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.030
and put the items in it side by side

31
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.020
in order.

32
00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.481
The first item starts at the address

33
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.800
where the block begins.

34
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.040
We call that the base address.

35
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.770
Say it is one thousand.

36
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.190
The next item starts four boxes later

37
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.430
at one thousand four.

38
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.040
The one after, at one thousand eight.

39
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.270
Do you see the pattern?

40
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.650
Item number i starts at the base address

41
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.580
plus i times the item size.

42
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.400
Item five hundred thousand: one thousand

43
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.960
plus four times five hundred thousand.

44
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.690
That is two million, one thousand.

45
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.099
That's one multiplication and one addition.

46
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.942
It takes exactly the same effort for item

47
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.370
five as for item five hundred thousand

48
00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.920
however long the array is.

49
00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.432
So reading or writing an item by

50
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:01.470
its index takes constant time, big-O of one.

51
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.120
There's a catch.

52
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.160
The block is reserved in advance

53
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.640
and the memory just after it may already

54
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.090
belong to something else.

55
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.810
So an array has a capacity

56
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.550
the number of boxes reserved

57
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.680
and that number is fixed.

58
00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.100
We also keep a size

59
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.260
how many of those slots hold real items.

60
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.320
Here the capacity is eight

61
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.390
and the size is five.

62
00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.911
What if we insert a new item

63
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.970
X at position two?

64
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.830
The items must stay side by side

65
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:33.730
with no gaps.

66
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.559
So C, D and E all have to move

67
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.800
one slot to the right, to make room.

68
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.260
We move the last one first.

69
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.350
If we moved C first, we would overwrite D.

70
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.790
E moves, then D, then C.

71
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.140
Now slot two is free, and X goes in.

72
00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.020
Three items moved.

73
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.680
Insert at the front and every item moves.

74
00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.060
Insert at the end and none do.

75
00:02:59.060 --> 00:02:59.960
In general

76
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.120
the number of items that move is the size

77
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.650
minus the position.

78
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.700
Removing is the mirror image.

79
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.720
Remove the item at position zero

80
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.550
and the hole it leaves must be closed.

81
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.970
Every item after it slides

82
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.770
one slot to the left

83
00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.110
starting with the first one

84
00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:19.300
until the gap is at the end.

85
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.780
Again: cheap at the end

86
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.530
expensive at the front.

87
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.910
Let's put it in a table.

88
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.970
Reading or writing by index: constant time.

89
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.740
This is what arrays are for.

90
00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.760
Inserting or removing in the

91
00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.047
middle or at the front

92
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.883
time proportional to the number

93
00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.650
of items that move, so up to n.

94
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.550
And the capacity is fixed

95
00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.270
so what happens when the array is full?

96
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.200
That is the next video.

97
00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.953
To sum up.

98
00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:50.597
An array keeps its items side

99
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.216
by side in one block

100
00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.676
so item i is at the base address

101
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.700
plus i times the item size.

102
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.930
That makes access by index constant time

103
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.524
and makes inserting or removing in the middle

104
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.250
cost a shift of everything after it.

105
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.149
Next, we'll put it in code and measure how

106
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.280
much those shifts really cost.

107
00:04:11.907 --> 00:04:14.257
Now let's build this array in Python

108
00:04:14.257 --> 00:04:16.867
and look at what its insert really costs.

109
00:04:17.447 --> 00:04:19.257
Here is our array.

110
00:04:19.257 --> 00:04:21.691
In Python, we can reserve a block of

111
00:04:21.757 --> 00:04:23.417
eight slots like this

112
00:04:23.417 --> 00:04:26.367
a list with eight None values.

113
00:04:26.717 --> 00:04:28.987
We keep two numbers: the capacity

114
00:04:28.987 --> 00:04:30.727
which is the length of that list

115
00:04:30.727 --> 00:04:32.167
and the size

116
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.147
how many slots are really in use.

117
00:04:34.147 --> 00:04:36.237
Right now it is zero.

118
00:04:36.587 --> 00:04:38.457
One thing to know about Python.

119
00:04:38.457 --> 00:04:41.617
A list does not hold the items themselves.

120
00:04:41.617 --> 00:04:43.512
Each slot holds a reference

121
00:04:43.577 --> 00:04:45.377
a small address that points to

122
00:04:45.437 --> 00:04:46.757
the item somewhere else.

123
00:04:46.757 --> 00:04:48.597
The slots are side by side

124
00:04:48.597 --> 00:04:50.777
so the address formula still works

125
00:04:50.777 --> 00:04:52.787
but it works on the references.

126
00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:55.037
Here is insert.

127
00:04:55.037 --> 00:04:56.477
First, two checks

128
00:04:56.477 --> 00:04:58.060
the array must not be full

129
00:04:58.117 --> 00:04:59.915
and the index must be between

130
00:04:59.977 --> 00:05:01.567
zero and the size.

131
00:05:01.917 --> 00:05:03.387
Then the loop.

132
00:05:03.387 --> 00:05:06.087
It starts at the first free slot

133
00:05:06.087 --> 00:05:09.443
and walks backwards, down to the index.

134
00:05:09.527 --> 00:05:12.390
Each step copies the item on its left

135
00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:15.027
into the slot on its right.

136
00:05:15.027 --> 00:05:18.334
That is the shift, and it runs from the end

137
00:05:18.407 --> 00:05:20.330
so nothing is overwritten

138
00:05:20.407 --> 00:05:21.917
before it has moved.

139
00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:25.097
When the loop is done, slot index is free

140
00:05:25.097 --> 00:05:27.077
so we write the new value there.

141
00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:28.787
And the size goes up by one.

142
00:05:29.367 --> 00:05:30.837
Remove mirrors it.

143
00:05:30.837 --> 00:05:32.699
We keep the value to return it.

144
00:05:32.757 --> 00:05:35.041
Then the loop copies each item

145
00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:36.457
one slot to the left

146
00:05:36.457 --> 00:05:38.527
starting from the removed index.

147
00:05:38.877 --> 00:05:40.067
At the end

148
00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:42.487
the last used slot is set back to None

149
00:05:42.487 --> 00:05:44.477
and the size goes down by one.

150
00:05:45.057 --> 00:05:46.125
Let's run it.

151
00:05:46.207 --> 00:05:48.198
We start with five letters in

152
00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:50.167
an array of capacity eight.

153
00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:52.217
The empty slots show None.

154
00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:55.777
Insert X at position two.

155
00:05:55.777 --> 00:05:58.437
C, D and E have moved right by one

156
00:05:58.437 --> 00:05:59.807
and the size is six.

157
00:06:00.157 --> 00:06:03.367
Remove position zero: A comes out

158
00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:04.847
everything slides left

159
00:06:04.847 --> 00:06:06.397
and the size is five again.

160
00:06:06.977 --> 00:06:08.445
Now the stopwatch.

161
00:06:08.527 --> 00:06:10.526
Here we insert at the front of an

162
00:06:10.587 --> 00:06:13.311
array of two thousand items and take

163
00:06:13.387 --> 00:06:15.457
the time of one insert.

164
00:06:15.807 --> 00:06:17.917
About forty-four microseconds.

165
00:06:17.917 --> 00:06:19.897
At sixteen thousand items

166
00:06:19.897 --> 00:06:21.057
eight times as many

167
00:06:21.057 --> 00:06:23.837
it is about four hundred.

168
00:06:23.837 --> 00:06:26.737
Eight times the items, nine times the time

169
00:06:26.737 --> 00:06:28.277
roughly proportional

170
00:06:28.277 --> 00:06:30.127
just as the count of shifts predicts.

171
00:06:30.477 --> 00:06:31.687
In the middle

172
00:06:31.687 --> 00:06:33.647
only half the items have to move

173
00:06:33.647 --> 00:06:35.547
so it takes about half as long

174
00:06:35.547 --> 00:06:38.437
but the time still grows with n.

175
00:06:39.017 --> 00:06:42.467
So in Python: items sit side by side

176
00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:44.453
indexing is constant time

177
00:06:44.527 --> 00:06:47.060
and every insert at the front pays for

178
00:06:47.127 --> 00:06:49.377
a shift of everything after it.

179
00:06:49.727 --> 00:06:50.477
Next time

180
00:06:50.477 --> 00:06:52.407
we'll deal with the fixed capacity.

